Joining a Linux System to a Domain: A Comprehensive Guide
In a corporate or enterprise environment, it is often necessary to integrate Linux systems into a domain. By joining a Linux system to a domain, administrators can manage user accounts, group policies, and security settings more efficiently across multiple systems. This blog post will provide a detailed overview of the fundamental concepts, usage methods, common practices, and best practices for joining a Linux system to a domain.

Fundamental Concepts#
Domain#
A domain is a logical grouping of computers, users, and resources that are managed as a single unit. In a Windows environment, Active Directory (AD) is the most common domain service. In a Linux environment, services like Samba can be used to create a domain-like environment, or Linux systems can be integrated with an existing Windows AD domain.

Authentication and Authorization#
When a Linux system joins a domain, it needs to be able to authenticate users against the domain's authentication service. This typically involves validating user credentials (username and password) and retrieving user information such as group memberships. Authorization determines what actions a user is allowed to perform on the system based on their group memberships.

Single Sign-On (SSO)#
Single Sign-On allows users to authenticate once and gain access to multiple resources within the domain without having to re-enter their credentials. This improves user experience and security by reducing the number of times passwords are entered.

Prerequisites#
Network Connectivity: The Linux system must be able to communicate with the domain controller over the network. This usually involves having the correct DNS settings configured to resolve the domain controller's hostname.
Administrative Privileges: You need administrative privileges on the Linux system to install packages and make system configuration changes.
Domain Credentials: You need valid domain administrator credentials to join the Linux system to the domain.

Usage Methods#
Using SSSD and Realmd#
SSSD (System Security Services Daemon) is a service that provides access to authentication and authorization mechanisms, and Realmd is a tool that simplifies the process of joining a system to a domain.

Step 1: Install Required Packages#
sudo apt update
sudo apt install realmd sssd sssd-tools libnss-sss libpam-sss adcli samba-common-bin oddjob oddjob-mkhomedir packagekit
Step 2: Discover the Domain#
sudo realm discover <domain_name>
Replace <domain_name> with the actual name of your domain, e.g., example.com.

Step 3: Join the Domain#
sudo realm join -U <domain_admin_username> <domain_name>
Replace <domain_admin_username> with the username of a domain administrator. You will be prompted to enter the password for the domain administrator account.

Manual Configuration with SSSD#
If you prefer to configure SSSD manually, you can follow these steps:

Step 1: Install SSSD#
sudo apt update
sudo apt install sssd sssd-tools libnss-sss libpam-sss
Step 2: Configure SSSD#
Edit the /etc/sssd/sssd.conf file and add the following configuration:

[sssd]
domains = <domain_name>
config_file_version = 2
services = nss, pam
 
[domain/<domain_name>]
ad_domain = <domain_name>
krb5_realm = <DOMAIN_NAME_IN_UPPERCASE>
realmd_tags = manages-system joined-with-adcli
cache_credentials = True
id_provider = ad
krb5_store_password_if_offline = True
default_shell = /bin/bash
ldap_id_mapping = True
use_fully_qualified_names = False
fallback_homedir = /home/%u
access_provider = ad
Replace <domain_name> with the actual name of your domain.

Step 3: Start and Enable SSSD#
sudo systemctl start sssd
sudo systemctl enable sssd
Step 4: Join the Domain#
sudo adcli join -U <domain_admin_username> <domain_name>

Common Practices#
Testing Domain Connectivity#
After joining the Linux system to the domain, you can test domain connectivity by checking if you can resolve domain user accounts.

id <domain_username>
Replace <domain_username> with the username of a domain user. If the command returns user information, it means the system can communicate with the domain and retrieve user information.

Managing User Accounts#
To allow domain users to log in to the Linux system, you need to configure PAM (Pluggable Authentication Modules) and NSS (Name Service Switch).

PAM Configuration#
Edit the /etc/pam.d/common-session file and add the following line:

session required pam_mkhomedir.so skel=/etc/skel umask=0077
This will create a home directory for domain users on their first login.

NSS Configuration#
Edit the /etc/nsswitch.conf file and modify the passwd, group, and shadow lines to include sss:

passwd:         files systemd sss
group:          files systemd sss
shadow:         files sss

Best Practices#
Security Considerations#
Use Encryption: Ensure that all communication between the Linux system and the domain controller is encrypted. This can be achieved by using Kerberos encryption, which is the default authentication protocol for Active Directory.
Limit Access: Only allow necessary users and services to access the domain resources. Use group policies to restrict access to sensitive files and directories.
Regularly Update Software: Keep the Linux system and all installed packages up to date to patch security vulnerabilities.
Regular Maintenance#
Monitor Logs: Regularly check the system logs for any authentication or authorization errors. The SSSD logs are located in /var/log/sssd/.
Renew Kerberos Tickets: Kerberos tickets have a limited lifespan. Configure the system to automatically renew Kerberos tickets to ensure continuous access to domain resources.

Conclusion#
Joining a Linux system to a domain can greatly enhance the manageability and security of your IT infrastructure. By following the steps outlined in this blog post, you can easily integrate your Linux systems into an existing domain. Whether you choose to use the automated method with Realmd or the manual configuration with SSSD, it is important to follow best practices to ensure the security and stability of your system.

